        "scope": "player_background",
        "excerpts": [
          "It hasn’t been all smooth sailing though. Alcaraz saved two match points against Fritz in the quarters, and found himself in a hole early at 1-2 0-40 today after three uncharacteristic forehand errors. He saved four break points in that game, and two other items of note played out that would also be themes for this match. The first is about volleying. Here’s a sentence I wrote in April after Shelton won Munich:",
          "Compare how tight to the baseline the white spread (missed shots) of Alcaraz is compared to Shelton on the forehand today:"
        ],
        "note": "读到 38 段",
        "match_verified": false
      },
      {
        "title": "Zverev vs Shelton: US Open Final Recap",
        "url": "https://hughclarke.substack.com/p/zverev-vs-shelton-us-open-final-recap",
        "published_at": "Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:30:41 GMT",
        "status": "read",
        "text_sha256": "96254bf195bcc7cedc12bb9be78e098760838ba9bfae3ec9ec87eea73bd0f5a7",
        "scope": "player_background",
        "excerpts": [
          "Both these men have great serves, but what has kept Zverev near the top of the game for nearly a decade is that he follows up his delivery with an elite baseline game. For 6’6”, the backhand, movement, and fitness are world-class, and the forehand has become braver when necessary. Here Shelton played a tactically perfect point, baiting Zverev with plenty of open court on the forehand down the line — a shot the German has historically hesitated to hit — but which he happily took on at 30-30 in an effort to maintain his break lead:",
          "Shelton did eventually break through in the third, breaking the Zverev serve for the first time in 60-odd service games, in part thanks to a double fault and a missed easy volley on set point from Zverev. You felt nerves must have played a part with the finish line so close."
        ],
        "note": "读到 48 段",
        "match_verified": false
      }
